---
title: "lock_management (Sui Whitepaper: Order Lock Lifecycle)"
---
stateDiagram-v2
    direction TB
    [*] --> q_obj_created: object created by transaction execution
    q_obj_created --> q_init_lock: Lock_v[ObjRef] = None (lock initialized)

    q_init_lock --> q_wait: object available for use in new transactions
    q_wait --> q_first_tx: first valid transaction Tx uses this ObjRef

    q_first_tx --> q_set_txsign: Lock_v[ObjRef] = TxSign (locked to Tx)
    q_set_txsign --> q_await_cert: awaiting certificate formation

    q_await_cert --> q_cert_arrives: certificate for same Tx arrives
    q_await_cert --> q_conflict_cert: certificate for different Tx arrives
    q_await_cert --> q_epoch_end: epoch ends without certificate

    q_cert_arrives --> q_set_cert: Lock_v[ObjRef] = TxCert
    q_set_cert --> q_execute: execute transaction, produce new object version
    q_execute --> q_new_version: new ObjRef with incremented version
    q_new_version --> q_init_lock

    q_conflict_cert --> q_disaster: BYZANTINE FAILURE DETECTED
    q_disaster --> [*]: halt and initiate disaster recovery with fraud proof

    q_epoch_end --> q_recover: object recovers at epoch boundary
    q_recover --> q_init_lock

    note right of q_set_txsign
        INV-001: Lock set to first valid transaction, no unlock within epoch
        INV-002: Conflicting certificates = proof that quorum honesty assumption violated
        INV-003: Lock update for ObjRef is atomic (strong key self-consistency)
        INV-004: Equivocating client locks are recoverable at epoch boundaries
    end note
